---
/**
 * The `page` template — website-generic scaffolding, one repeatable layout. Contact and
 * Consulting today; About, Colophon, Apologia and Projects follow.
 *
 * ── THE TWO DOCUMENTS EXERCISE DIFFERENT HALVES OF IT ───────────────────────
 *
 * Contact shipped the first pass and used almost none of the type: no `heroImg`, no
 * `bodyText`, `bandCopy: false`. Consulting uses all three, which is why it is what
 * finished the template rather than a second instance of it.
 *
 *   Contact      h1, lede, the band with its copy suppressed
 *   Consulting   h1, lede, 19 body blocks with h2s and h3s, a hero, the band WITH copy
 *
 * So every rule below has to produce both, and the interesting cases are the ones where
 * a row is simply absent.
 *
 * ── THE FRAME IS reviews.astro's, RAIL GYMNASTICS INCLUDED NOW ──────────────
 *
 * The first pass said this page was "reviews.astro's frame MINUS the rail gymnastics",
 * because nothing was rendered in the rail. Consulting renders two things in it, so the
 * whole mechanism is needed after all: the transparent <PageHeader> that lets the rail
 * align to the LEDE rather than to the h1, the zeroed row gap that goes with it, and the
 * explicit rows that make the rail's `-1` mean anything.
 *
 * Both boards draw that alignment — the desktop board puts the h1 above a two-column
 * region whose left column opens with the lede, and the rail's top edge is level with the
 * lede's first line, 16 below the title.
 *
 * ── `bodyText` IS PROSE IN THE MAIN COLUMN, NOT <PageHeader>'s THIRD SLOT ───
 *
 * That slot is documented as "a short paragraph of intro copy under the lead" and is what
 * Reviews passes a single block to. Consulting's 19 blocks include four headings, so
 * routing them through it would put h2s and h3s inside <header> with no rhythm ramp to
 * space them — `.page-body` covers `p + p` and nothing else.
 *
 * As `.detail` they take the ramp instead, and the ramp turns out to be exactly what the
 * board draws: 24 between blocks, 64 before an h2, 48 before an h3, 16 after any heading.
 * Verified gap by gap against the desktop board. Nothing new was needed for body rhythm,
 * which is the useful result — the same finding the article page reported.
 *
 * ── THE HERO IS RELOCATED, NOT HIDDEN, AND THAT DRIVES THE DOM ORDER ────────
 *
 * The rail at md; a full-bleed band below it. "Consulting — Mobile" (757:1525) draws it as
 * its own band between the header and the body, 375x375 — so reading order is
 * h1 -> lede -> hero -> body -> band, and the DOM is authored in exactly that order with
 * the grid moving the hero into the rail at md. Source order is reading order, which is
 * the rule the footer and the article entry already follow; no `order` is involved.
 *
 * The cost is that the <aside> precedes the body in the DOM at md too. Accepted: "On This
 * Page" before the content it indexes is useful rather than inverted, and the alternative
 * is an `order` swap on a container holding a link list, which the article page's own note
 * rules out for anything focusable.
 *
 * <aside> AND NOT <div>, and it costs no landmark. An unnamed <aside> maps to
 * `complementary` only when its nearest sectioning ancestor is <body>; this one sits inside
 * <Band as="section">, so it maps to `generic` and the <nav> inside stays the one landmark
 * a screen-reader user navigates by.
 *
 * ── THE RAIL IS STILL RESERVED BY ABSENCE ON A PAGE THAT HAS NOTHING FOR IT ─
 *
 * On desktop a `page` always leaves columns 9-12 standing (Andy, 2026-09-04), and the
 * reason is measure: the prose column is `--col-main` (656), near DESIGN.md's 66ch target,
 * where `--col-full` (996) would push well past it.
 *
 * Contact has no hero and no h2s, so the <aside> is not rendered at all and 9-12 are empty
 * by definition. THERE IS STILL NO EMPTY <aside> HERE — an unnamed `complementary`
 * wrapping nothing would be worse than no element, and the guard on the element is what
 * keeps that true as pages are added.
 *
 * ── BANDS ARE OPT-IN, SO A NULL IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A GAP ─────────────
 *
 * `pageBands` lists the bands a page carries at all (Andy, 2026-09-04). A band missing
 * from that list resolves to null even when Settings defines a default, which inverts the
 * ladder's usual "the default always wins if nothing else does". So the guard below is not
 * defensive coding — null is the editor having said no.
 *
 * PLACED AS BODY CONTENT inside the page's single band, at `--col-main`, because that is
 * what both boards draw: the form sits in the prose column beside the rail, not in a band
 * of its own.
 *
 * A comment here used to speculate that `bandCopy` might discriminate between this
 * in-flow placement and an appended full-bleed one, and wondered what a page taking the
 * Settings default would want. RESOLVED BY CONSULTING, which is that page: `bandCopy` is
 * true, the band renders its own "Get In Touch" heading, and it sits in exactly the same
 * position Contact's copy-suppressed form does. Placement is identical either way, and
 * `bandCopy` controls only whether the band carries its own copy.
 */
import {loadQuery} from '../sanity/load-query'
import {
  PAGE_SLUGS_QUERY,
  PAGE_QUERY,
  PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY,
  PAGE_WORK_WITH_ME_BAND_QUERY,
} from '../sanity/queries/pages'
import {focalPoint} from '../sanity/image'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Band from '../components/Band.astro'
import Grid from '../components/Grid.astro'
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import Prose from '../components/prose/Prose.astro'
import RailNav from '../components/RailNav.astro'
import SanityImage from '../components/SanityImage.astro'
import GetInTouchBand from '../components/bands/GetInTouchBand.astro'
import WorkWithMeBand from '../components/bands/WorkWithMeBand.astro'
import {GET_IN_TOUCH_HEADING, GET_IN_TOUCH_ID} from '../components/bands/getInTouch'
import {headingLinks} from '../components/prose/headingId'
import {pageNode} from '../lib/linked-data'

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const rows = await loadQuery(PAGE_SLUGS_QUERY)
  /* The query filters on `defined(slug.current)`, but TypeGen types a projected slug as
     `string | null` because it cannot see the filter — and a null would become the
     literal path `/null`. Narrowing rather than asserting, as insights/[slug] does. */
  return rows
    .filter((row): row is {params: {slug: string}} => Boolean(row.params.slug))
    .map((row) => ({params: row.params}))
}

/* Three queries rather than one projection, and not by preference — folding a band into
   PAGE_QUERY silently untypes it to `any`, with nothing in this file to reveal it. See
   PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY for the mechanism and the probe that caught it, and
   PAGE_WORK_WITH_ME_BAND_QUERY for why the two bands stay apart from each other too.
   All three are independent, so they run concurrently. */
const {slug} = Astro.params
const [page, bands, workBands] = await Promise.all([
  loadQuery(PAGE_QUERY, {slug}),
  loadQuery(PAGE_GET_IN_TOUCH_BAND_QUERY, {slug}),
  loadQuery(PAGE_WORK_WITH_ME_BAND_QUERY, {slug}),
])

/* Loud rather than quiet. Every slug here came out of the dataset a moment ago, so a miss
   means the two queries disagree — which should fail the build rather than ship a page
   with an empty body. */
if (!page) throw new Error(`[[slug]] no page document for slug "${slug}"`)

/**
 * ── "On This Page" — h2s ONLY, PLUS THE BAND ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The h3s are omitted deliberately: the board lists three entries and "Information
 * architecture" is not among them. Consulting's two h2s and the band are exactly those
 * three.
 *
 * `flatMap` with a 0-or-1 array rather than `filter().map()`, matching the article page:
 * it narrows and projects in one pass, so `block.style` is only reached where TypeScript
 * has already established the block is a text block rather than an image.
 *
 * LABELS COME THROUGH VERBATIM AND NOTHING REWRITES CASING (Andy, 2026-09-06). Title case
 * is an editorial rule enforced in the Studio; a heading arriving in the wrong case should
 * show in the wrong case in the rail, because that is the signal the content needs fixing.
 */
const bodyContents = headingLinks(page.bodyText, 'h2')

/**
 * The band's heading joins the list — but ONLY when the band actually renders one.
 *
 * With `bandCopy` false there is no h2 and therefore no `#get-in-touch` anchor, so an
 * entry for it would be a link to nothing. It would also LOOK fine: a fragment with no
 * target scrolls nowhere rather than erroring. Contact is that case.
 *
 * ── WORK WITH ME IS EXCLUDED, AND A PAGE NOW ACTUALLY CARRIES IT ────────────
 *
 * `pageBands` offers both and Projects uses Work With Me, so this is a live exclusion
 * rather than a hypothetical one (Andy, 2026-09-06). In light mode Work With Me uses a
 * different background and reads as a separate part of the page, where Get in Touch shares
 * the page ground and is the page's natural call to action — so Get in Touch belongs in
 * the outline with the other headings and Work With Me does not.
 *
 * It falls out of the structure rather than needing a filter: only the Get in Touch band
 * is appended to `bodyContents` below. The rule is a fact about the two bands rather than
 * about any one page, which is why it is written here and not left to be rediscovered.
 */
const contents = bands?.touchBand?.bandCopy
  ? [...bodyContents, {id: GET_IN_TOUCH_ID, label: GET_IN_TOUCH_HEADING}]
  : bodyContents

/**
 * ── ONE FILE SHAPE, TWO BOXES, AND THE CROP IS WHY ───────────────────────────
 *
 * The hero is 1:1 full bleed below md and 4:5 in the rail from md (Andy, 2026-09-07), and
 * both boards now draw those exactly: 375x375 and 231x288.75.
 *
 * NO `aspect` IS PASSED, which is the decision worth understanding. `imageAttrs` builds
 * one srcset for one shape — `srcset` varies resolution, never framing — so two aspects
 * would mean two calls and a <picture> with `<source media>`, i.e. breakpoints written
 * into an HTML attribute that cannot read a token. <SanityImage>'s own header names that
 * as the thing to avoid.
 *
 * Instead the file arrives at the editor's CROPPED shape and CSS reframes it twice, with
 * `object-fit: cover` absorbing the difference — the same arrangement <SanityHero> uses.
 * It is nearly free here because the crop is already 549x938 -> 549x693, about 4:5: at md
 * the file is the box's shape, and at the base only height is discarded.
 *
 * ── THE FOCUS POINT NEEDS THE COORDINATE TRANSFORM, NOT THE RAW HOTSPOT ─────
 *
 * `focalPoint` converts the hotspot out of the ORIGINAL asset's coordinate space and into
 * the served file's. It matters more here than anywhere else in the build: this crop
 * removes 56.5% of the width from the left, so the raw hotspot would read 78.1% where
 * 49.6% is correct — a 28-point error that puts the subject out of frame at 1:1. See the
 * function's own note.
 *
 * A custom property rather than an inline `object-position`, so the property name and its
 * default stay in the stylesheet and only the DATA arrives from the document.
 */
const heroFocus = focalPoint(page.heroImg ?? {})
const hasHero = Boolean(page.heroImg?.asset)

/* Only the two slugs whose schema.org type says more than `WebPage` does. `/apologia`,
   `/consulting` and `/projects` get no page node at all — see pageNode for why a generic
   one is ceremony. Keyed on slug, which is what distinguishes these pages; a `page`
   carries no field that could say it another way. */
const SPECIFIC_PAGE_TYPES = {about: 'AboutPage', contact: 'ContactPage'} as const
const pageType = SPECIFIC_PAGE_TYPES[slug as keyof typeof SPECIFIC_PAGE_TYPES]
if (!Astro.site) throw new Error('[pages/[slug]] `site` is unset in astro.config.mjs')
const jsonLd = pageType
  ? [
      pageNode({
        site: Astro.site,
        url: new URL(`/${slug}/`, Astro.site).href,
        type: pageType,
        name: page.title,
        description: page.description,
      }),
    ]
  : undefined
---

{
  /*
    ── 64 TOP, AND THE BOTTOM DEPENDS ON WHAT FOLLOWS ─────────────────────────

    The Contact board's BodyBand is 811 tall with its content frame at y=64 and 651 tall,
    leaving 96 below; the Consulting board draws 64/96 on the same band. `space="band"`
    supplies the 64 by default and only the end is overridden, taking `--rhythm-section` —
    the token every other 96 in the build uses — rather than a literal, so both clamp
    together at narrow widths.

    THE 96 IS NOT ABOUT THIS BAND, IT IS ABOUT THE FOOTER. Reviews draws the same
    asymmetry and for the same stated reason: the page has nothing between its content and
    the footer, and the extra 32 is what that gap wants.

    ── WHICH STOPS BEING TRUE THE MOMENT AN APPENDED BAND FOLLOWS ─────────────

    Projects carries Work With Me, and that band brings its own `space="section"` — 96
    above its own content. Leaving `spaceEnd="section"` here would put 96 + 96 between the
    body copy and the band's heading. So the override is conditional: 96 when this band
    runs into the footer, 64 when something else follows it.

    64 IS NOT A GUESS. It is what the article page's content band takes at both edges
    (`.article-grid { padding-block: var(--rhythm-band) }`) precisely because appended
    bands follow it there — the same situation, already settled.

    Do NOT try to do this from a page rule instead: `.band[data-space='…']` compiles with
    <Band>'s own scope attribute to (0,3,0) and a page reaching the root gets (0,2,0), so
    it would lose wherever it sat in the source. `spaceEnd` exists for exactly this.
  */
}
<BaseLayout
  title={page.title ?? 'Page'}
  description={page.description ?? undefined}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Masthead />

  <main>
    <Band as="section" spaceEnd={workBands?.workBand ? 'band' : 'section'}>
      <Grid>
        <PageHeader title={page.title ?? ''} lede={page.lede} />

        {
          /*
            ── THE RAIL: A HERO THAT SCROLLS AND A NAV THAT STICKS ────────────

            Rendered only if it has something in it, so Contact emits no element. Both
            children are independently optional: a page could have a hero and no h2s, or
            h2s and no hero.

            STICKINESS IS THE WRAPPER'S, NEVER THE RAIL'S. "On This Page" pins and the
            hero scrolls away with the content (Andy, 2026-09-06), which is precisely the
            arrangement the article page's rail was built to allow — its note calls this
            page out by name: "on Services the image is an ordinary sibling above the
            sticky child and scrolls away by itself". One `.rail-sticky` around the nav
            alone, and the hero above it needs no rule at all.

            THAT MAKES `.rail` A STRETCHED GRID ITEM, AND IT MUST STAY ONE. Do not add
            `align-self: start`: a sticky element travels only inside its containing
            block, so the rail's box has to be as tall as the prose beside it. The
            failure is silent — the nav simply scrolls away like anything else. Reviews
            does the opposite because there the aside IS the sticky element.
          */
        }
        {
          (hasHero || contents.length > 0) && (
            <aside class="rail">
              {hasHero && (
                <div class="hero-frame">
                  {/*
                    THE FRAME EXISTS TO BE MEASURED. A container query never restyles its
                    own container, so the element declaring `container-type` cannot be the
                    one that responds to it — TopicList and ArticleCard both record the
                    same wall. Its width is exactly the hero's, which is what lets the
                    query below ask the only question that matters: has the image detached
                    from the viewport edges?

                    `alt` is not passed: <SanityImage> falls back to the asset's own
                    `altText` and warns when there is none, which is the behaviour we want
                    rather than an empty string asserted here. Consulting's hero has one.

                    NO `sizes` EITHER. The default for a lazy image is `auto, 100vw`, and
                    `auto` measures the real laid-out box — 375 or less at the base, 231 in
                    the rail — so the layout stays the only description of the layout.
                  */}
                  <SanityImage
                    image={page.heroImg!}
                    class="page-hero"
                    style={heroFocus ? `--page-hero-focus: ${heroFocus}` : undefined}
                  />
                </div>
              )}

              {contents.length > 0 && (
                <div class="rail-sticky">
                  <RailNav items={contents} id="rail-contents" />
                </div>
              )}
            </aside>
          )
        }

        {
          /*
            GUARDED, AND IT HAS TO BE. <Prose> renders nothing from a null array, but the
            wrapper would still be a grid item — an empty row carrying its own top margin.
            Measured on Contact, which has no `bodyText`: the lede-to-band gap came out at
            88 instead of 64, the extra 24 being an invisible `.detail` row between them.
            Nothing on screen says why, which is why this is a guard and not a comment.
          */
        }
        {
          page.bodyText && (
            <div class="detail">
              <Prose value={page.bodyText} />
            </div>
          )
        }

        {
          /*
            `aria-labelledby` is NOT set on anything here. With `bandCopy` false there is
            no heading to point at, and with it true the h2 sits inside the page's own
            main region rather than in a band of its own — so there is no separate region
            to name. RssBand puts the relationship on the <Band> it owns; this component
            owns none, and the naming goes with the frame.
          */
        }
        {
          bands?.touchBand && (
            <GetInTouchBand
              class="page-touch-band"
              message={bands.touchBand.message}
              bandCopy={bands.touchBand.bandCopy}
            />
          )
        }
      </Grid>
    </Band>

    {
      /*
        ── APPENDED, NOT PLACED IN THE PROSE COLUMN ───────────────────────────

        OUTSIDE the content <Band>, unlike <GetInTouchBand>, and the two are not
        inconsistent — they are different kinds of thing. This component owns its own
        <Band tone="logo" space="section"> and <Grid>, so nesting it inside the content
        band would put a full-bleed element inside a grid item and pay the band padding
        twice. Get in Touch owns no frame precisely so it CAN sit in the column.

        That difference is the design, not an implementation detail: `tone="logo"` lifts
        this band off the page ground in both themes, which is what makes it read as a
        separate part of the page — and is the reason it stays out of the rail's "On This
        Page" while Get in Touch appears there. See the note on `contents` above.

        It supplies its own <Band>, so the page states no spacing for it. What the page
        does own is the band ABOVE it — see the note on `spaceEnd`.

        Rendered only if the ladder produced one. `pageBands` is an opt-in gate, so null
        means the editor did not list this band, and a heading over an empty band is worse
        than no band. Matches the guard on Get in Touch.
      */
    }
    {
      workBands?.workBand && (
        <WorkWithMeBand
          message={workBands.workBand.message}
          clientLogos={workBands.workBand.clientLogos}
        />
      )
    }
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ── The frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * ── THE HEADER IS TRANSPARENT, SO THE RAIL CAN START AT THE LEDE ───────────
   *
   * A rail can only align to a row that exists. With <PageHeader> as one grid item the
   * only row available is the header's, whose top is the h1; `display: contents` turns
   * its two children into two grid ROWS, and the rail then starts from the second. The
   * component ships the `.page-intro` wrapper for exactly this.
   *
   * `--page-header-span` is not set, so the header's children take their default
   * `--col-main` — correct here, because this page has a rail.
   *
   * ── AND THE ROW GAP HAS TO GO TO ZERO, WHICH IS THE PRICE ──────────────────
   *
   * <Grid>'s row gap is `rhythm-heading-major` (64), correct for its usual job of
   * separating whole bands of content and wrong between a title and its own lede, which
   * want 16. Once the header is transparent that boundary IS a grid row boundary, and a
   * gap cannot vary per row — so it is zeroed and every row states its own top margin.
   * Five relationships, each named, none of them a remainder:
   *
   *     h1 -> .page-intro     16, from <PageHeader> (unchanged in flow or in grid)
   *     .page-intro -> .rail  aligned, because both take `heading-close`
   *     .page-intro -> hero   48, base only — the hero is in the rail from md
   *     -> .detail            48 below the hero at the base, 24 below the lede from md
   *     -> .page-touch-band   64, because a band opens with a heading
   *
   * This is a page-level decision about a shared component's spacing, so it is scoped to
   * this page's <Grid> and nothing else inherits it.
   */
  .grid {
    row-gap: 0;

    /*
     * ── THE ROWS ARE EXPLICIT SO THE RAIL'S `-1` MEANS SOMETHING ─────────────
     *
     * Negative grid lines count from the end of the EXPLICIT grid, and a grid with no
     * `grid-template-rows` has exactly one row line — so `grid-row: 2 / -1` would resolve
     * its end to line 1, the browser would swap the reversed pair, and the rail would
     * silently span row 1 instead of everything below it. Reviews hit this first.
     *
     * FOUR is the main column's row count from md: h1, lede, body, band. Consulting uses
     * all four and Contact three, so Contact gets a zero-height fourth row — free, under
     * `row-gap: 0`. Below md the hero is in the flow too and takes a fifth, implicit row,
     * which costs nothing because `-1` is only used inside the md query.
     */
    grid-template-rows: repeat(4, auto);
  }

  .page-header {
    display: contents;
  }

  .detail,
  .page-touch-band {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }

  .page-touch-band {
    margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-heading-major);
  }

  /*
   * 48 below the hero at the base. From md the hero has left the flow and the lede sits
   * directly above, which wants `rhythm-paragraph` instead — see the md query. The board
   * draws the lede and the first paragraph as one Body Group with a 24 gap, so they read
   * as continuous prose rather than as two sections.
   */
  .detail {
    margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-block);
  }

  /*
   * ── EVERY RAIL TARGET TAKES THE SAME SCROLL OFFSET ─────────────────────────
   *
   * Both h2s the rail can jump to — the body's and the band's — so a heading arrives
   * `rhythm-band` below the viewport edge rather than flush against it, with the same
   * breathing room the bands themselves have. `rhythm-band` is what reviews.astro
   * already gives its own rail targets and what the sticky rail uses for its `top`.
   *
   * ── IT WAS REMOVED AND THEN PUT BACK, AND THE COST IS REAL ────────────────
   *
   * Dropped briefly on 2026-09-07 in favour of landing flush, which exposes more content
   * below the heading, and because the offset aligns the h2's TOP with the top of "On
   * This Page" while their BASELINES do not meet — near-alignment, which the eye reads
   * as a miss rather than as alignment.
   *
   * REINSTATED the same day (Andy): consistency across the three rail-bearing page types
   * is worth more than that baseline offset. So the offset is a deliberate choice with a
   * known cost, not an unexamined default — and the cost is recorded here so that
   * whoever revisits it starts from the argument rather than rediscovering it.
   *
   * A value that made the baselines meet would be derived from two line boxes rather
   * than named, and would have to change with either font. That is the trade being
   * declined, not overlooked.
   *
   * `:global()` because these h2s belong to <Prose> and to <GetInTouchBand>: a scope
   * attribute reaches a component's ROOT and never its descendants.
   */
  .detail :global(h2),
  .page-touch-band :global(h2) {
    scroll-margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-band);
  }

  /* ── The rail, below md: IT DISPERSES RATHER THAN COLLAPSING ──────────────── */
  /*
   * `display: contents` erases a box while keeping its children, so `.rail` and
   * `.rail-sticky` stop being layout at all and the hero becomes a grid item of <Grid> in
   * its own right — free to place itself full bleed between the lede and the body, which
   * is a position no collapsing rail could reach. <RailNav> removes itself.
   *
   * This is the mechanism the article page built and predicted this page would need. Both
   * elements are plain containers with no role, so the historic `display: contents`
   * accessibility bug has nothing to take.
   */
  .rail,
  .rail-sticky {
    display: contents;
  }

  /* ── The hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * ── FULL BLEED TO 500px, THEN CAPPED AND CENTRED — WITH NO BREAKPOINT ──────
   *
   * A 1:1 image running full bleed all the way to md would be an uncomfortable amount of
   * picture on a wide phone (Andy, 2026-09-07), so it detaches from the page margin and
   * centres once it reaches its cap. The cap IS the switch point, which is why this needs
   * no query: below 500 the width wins and the image bleeds, above it the cap wins and the
   * remainder becomes margin. Same shape as <SanityHero>'s `max-block-size`, and correct
   * for the same reason — a derived switch point cannot be out of step with itself.
   *
   * ── WHY THE BLEED IS A WIDTH AND NOT A NEGATIVE MARGIN ─────────────────────
   *
   * `margin-inline: calc(var(--grid-margin) * -1)` is the usual way to escape a grid's
   * inset and it was the plan, but it cannot be combined with a centred cap: with
   * `justify-self: stretch` a binding `max-width` pins the item to the START edge, and
   * with `justify-self: center` an <img> at `width: auto` shrink-wraps to its intrinsic
   * width instead of filling. Moving the arithmetic into the width lets one declaration
   * pair serve both states, and `justify-self: center` then handles the overflow
   * symmetrically — centring an item larger than its area overflows it equally on both
   * sides, into <Grid>'s own padding rather than past the viewport.
   *
   * ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   * │  THE `100%` INSIDE `--grid-margin` RESOLVES AGAINST THIS ELEMENT'S GRID  │
   * │  AREA, NOT THE GRID'S WIDTH — so this is only exactly right because the  │
   * │  token is `max(16px, …)` and that floor governs everywhere below a 1028  │
   * │  viewport, while this rule is scoped below md (768). Both sides read 16.  │
   * │  It breaks if md moves above 1028 or if `--grid-content` shrinks.         │
   * └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   *
   * `--col-full` explicitly, because auto-placement would give this a ONE-COLUMN track:
   * the h1 and the lede occupy rows 1 and 2 at `1 / -1`, so an item with no column
   * position lands in row 3 spanning a single column.
   */
  .hero-frame {
    /* 500px. Local rather than a token — one consumer, and DESIGN.md names no such
       width. Same call as the client logo's `--logo-size`. */
    --page-hero-max: 31.25rem;

    grid-column: var(--col-full);
    inline-size: min(calc(100% + 2 * var(--grid-margin)), var(--page-hero-max));
    justify-self: center;
    margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-block);

    /* What makes the radius query below possible, and it contains the inline axis, which
       is what we want: the frame takes its width from the rule above, never from the
       image inside it. */
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  .page-hero {
    inline-size: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;

    /* Fills the box, which is a different shape from the file at both breakpoints.
       `object-position` aims the crop; see the note on `focalPoint` in the frontmatter for
       why the value cannot be the raw hotspot. */
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: var(--page-hero-focus, 50% 50%);
  }

  /*
   * ── THE RADIUS APPEARS WHEN THE IMAGE STOPS TOUCHING THE VIEWPORT ──────────
   *
   * At full bleed it carries none, and DESIGN.md's Elevation section gives the reason for
   * a full-width band that generalises to this: it "carries no radius and no border — the
   * viewport edges do the containing". Once the frame hits its cap there are no viewport
   * edges doing that job, so the image takes `radius-1`, which Shapes gives content
   * images. Confirmed against the boards (Andy, 2026-09-07).
   *
   * A SIZE CONTAINER QUERY RATHER THAN A MEDIA QUERY, which is what keeps tokens.css the
   * only place a viewport width is written. It is also the more accurate question: this
   * asks about the space the component occupies, which is what DESIGN.md nominates size
   * queries for, and it would stay correct if the frame were ever placed somewhere else.
   *
   * THE THRESHOLD IS A LITERAL AND HAS TO BE. A query condition is evaluated outside any
   * element's style context, so `var(--page-hero-max)` cannot resolve there — the same
   * constraint that put the flags in tokens.css in the first place, and the same
   * duplication TopicList carries with its `16rem`. The two numbers are coupled: if the
   * cap moves, this moves with it.
   *
   * FALSE AT md, where the frame is 231px — so the rail's radius is set in the md query
   * below rather than inherited from here. Two rules for two different reasons, each
   * stated positively, with no ordering dependency between them.
   */
  @container (inline-size >= 31.25rem) {
    .page-hero {
      border-radius: var(--radius-1);
    }
  }

  /* ── From md: the rail becomes a column ──────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * `grid-row: 2 / -1` starts the rail at the LEDE's row, which is the alignment both
   * boards draw, and runs it to the end so the sticky nav has the page's full height to
   * travel. `margin-block-start` then matches the 16 <PageHeader> puts between the h1 and
   * the intro, so the two columns begin on the same line.
   *
   * `top` on the sticky child is a judgment the boards cannot show, since they draw no
   * scroll position. `rhythm-band` is what the article rail settled on for the same
   * reason: the masthead does not stick, so `top: 0` would pin the nav flush against the
   * viewport edge.
   *
   * 48 from the hero to the nav — the board draws the image 288.75 tall with the nav
   * frame at 336.75. `heading-minor` rather than `rhythm-block` because what follows is a
   * heading; both are 48, and DESIGN.md's rule is to pick by prefix and name, never by
   * value.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .rail {
      display: block;
      grid-column: var(--col-rail);
      grid-row: 2 / -1;
      margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    }

    .rail-sticky {
      display: block;
      position: sticky;
      top: var(--rhythm-band);
    }

    /*
     * The frame is no longer a grid item, so `grid-column` and `justify-self` go inert on
     * their own and only these two need unsaying: the width becomes the rail column's,
     * and the row margin gives way to `.rail`'s.
     */
    .hero-frame {
      inline-size: 100%;
      margin-block-start: 0;
    }

    .page-hero {
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
      border-radius: var(--radius-1);
    }

    .hero-frame + .rail-sticky {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-minor);
    }

    /* 24 now, not 48: the hero has moved to the rail and the lede sits directly above. */
    .detail {
      margin-block-start: var(--rhythm-paragraph);
    }
  }
